 "news": {
  "date": "2026-10-21",
  "asof": "2026-10-22 09:50 UTC",
  "headline": "Meta raised its capex floor and was paid for it \u2014 and France crossed five percent without moving an American yield",
  "standfirst": "A broad rally rather than another rotation: the Nasdaq rose 1.05%, the Dow 0.69% and the S&P 0.58% to within reach of its October record, with the semiconductor index +1.97% and technology the leading sector; only the Russell lagged, at +0.27%. SpaceX rose 7.79% on its first profit as a public company, $4.4bn on revenue up 161% to $14.6bn, and Tesla 3.41% on merger talk, while Netflix fell 5.31% to a 52-week low on weak Q4 guidance. After the close Meta reported revenue up 26% to $64.60bn, guided Q4 to $73-76bn and raised the floor of its 2026 capex range to $135bn from $130bn \u2014 on negative free cash flow of $2.12bn \u2014 and rose about 3% after hours. Meanwhile France's ten-year crossed 5% for the first time since 2002, with Carrefour down nearly 14% on riot damage, a Paris march called for Saturday and Moody's reviewing on Friday \u2014 and the US thirty-year closed unchanged at 5.42%, the ten-year +1bp to 5.13%, the two-year +2bp to 4.81%. Crude fell despite a 3.06m-barrel draw: WTI $97.15, Brent $108.82, after Russia agreed to resume US-mediated Ukraine talks. In the book OKTA rose 7.54% to 208.64, its largest gain in any full session since entry, and AAPL 1.32% to a record close, while GOOG fell 1.62% to its lowest since 10 September after Pichai said Google Cloud is giving up revenue for AI capacity. A net of +13.66. Heat 3.05R against 3.0R, unchanged for a thirtieth session. 2 of 3 positions are now above +1R.",
  "themes": [
   {
    "kicker": "What's next",
    "right": "two events Friday \u00b7 then the Fed and Alphabet",
    "title": "Friday carries the foldable and a French rating decision",
    "items": [
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>iPhone Duo first shipments</b> and retail availability in the US and 70-plus countries, 28 more on the 30th \u2014 now with a two-per-customer limit, so the test is how little stock exists",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-23",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FRA",
      "html": "<b>Moody's reviews France</b> at Aa3 negative with the ten-year above 5% \u2014 a one-notch cut takes it to A1, still investment grade",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-23",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FOMC \u00b7 GOOG",
      "html": "<b>Fed decision and Alphabet Q3 on the same afternoon</b> \u2014 a hold is expected, December is 94% priced for a hike, and Alphabet carries $195-205bn of capex into it after Pichai's cloud remarks",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>Apple Q4</b> \u2014 a question about mix and margin after the Loop downgrade and a record close, with the foldable rationed and the Pro's China lead times at zero",
      "when": "Mon \u00b7 2026-11-02",
      "kind": "calendar"
     }
    ],
    "note": "<b>Friday is two unrelated events and the second one is the larger.</b> The <b>Duo reaches shops on the 23rd</b> in the US and more than seventy countries, with 28 more on the 30th, now under a <b>two-unit purchase limit</b> \u2014 which has already answered the demand question and turned Friday into a read on supply. Then <b>Moody's reviews France</b>, rated <b>Aa3 negative</b>, with the ten-year <b>above 5% for the first time since 2002</b> and a <b>Yellow Vests march in Paris on Saturday</b>. A one-notch cut takes France to A1. Two consecutive sessions have now shown US yields ignoring French deterioration, so the honest expectation is that a downgrade does nothing here either \u2014 but a downgrade is a different kind of event from a protest, and it is the cleanest test the thesis will get."
   }
  ],
  "ondeck": "<b>Friday is two unrelated events and the second one is the larger.</b> The <b>Duo reaches shops on the 23rd</b> in the US and more than seventy countries, with 28 more on the 30th, now under a <b>two-unit purchase limit</b> \u2014 which has already answered the demand question and turned Friday into a read on supply. Then <b>Moody's reviews France</b>, rated <b>Aa3 negative</b>, with the ten-year <b>above 5% for the first time since 2002</b> and a <b>Yellow Vests march in Paris on Saturday</b>. A one-notch cut takes France to A1. Two consecutive sessions have now shown US yields ignoring French deterioration, so the honest expectation is that a downgrade does nothing here either \u2014 but a downgrade is a different kind of event from a protest, and it is the cleanest test the thesis will get.",
